## Main SDK client class

The main class you must instantiate is **`GraphServiceClient`** (namespace `Microsoft.Graph`, package `Microsoft.Graph`).

## Typical C# example calling `/me`

```csharp
using Azure.Identity;
using Microsoft.Graph;

// 1. Set up authentication (example: interactive browser / delegated auth)
var scopes = new[] { "User.Read" };

var options = new InteractiveBrowserCredentialOptions
{
    TenantId = "common",
    ClientId = "YOUR_CLIENT_ID",
    AuthorityHost = AzureAuthorityHosts.AzurePublicCloud,
    RedirectUri = new Uri("http://localhost")
};

var interactiveCredential = new InteractiveBrowserCredential(options);

// 2. Instantiate the main SDK client class
var graphClient = new GraphServiceClient(interactiveCredential, scopes);

// 3. Call the /me endpoint
var user = await graphClient.Me.GetAsync();

Console.WriteLine($"Display Name: {user?.DisplayName}");
Console.WriteLine($"Mail: {user?.Mail}");
```

Key points:
- `GraphServiceClient` is constructed with a `TokenCredential` (from `Azure.Identity`, e.g., `InteractiveBrowserCredential`, `ClientSecretCredential`, `DeviceCodeCredential`, etc.) plus the required delegated scopes.
- Calling `/me` requires **delegated permissions** (like `User.Read`) and a signed-in user context — it cannot be called with pure application/client-credentials flow since there's no "me" for an app-only token.
- The fluent API `graphClient.Me.GetAsync()` maps to a `GET https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/me` request under the hood.

