## Calling Microsoft Graph `/me` from C# using the Microsoft Graph SDK

### Main SDK client class to instantiate
**`GraphServiceClient`** (from the `Microsoft.Graph` NuGet package) is the main entry point/client class you must instantiate to call Microsoft Graph from C#.

### Example: Creating the client and calling `/me`

```csharp
using Microsoft.Graph;

// Create a new instance of GraphServiceClient (requires a TokenCredential / auth provider)
GraphServiceClient graphClient = new GraphServiceClient(tokenCredential);

// GET https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/me
var user = await graphClient.Me.GetAsync();
```
*(Source: [learn.microsoft.com/graph/sdks/create-requests](https://learn.microsoft.com/graph/sdks/create-requests#read-information-from-microsoft-graph))*

### Example: Using it inside an ASP.NET Core MVC controller (typical DI pattern)

```csharp
using Microsoft.Graph;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

[Authorize]
public class ProfileController : Controller
{
    private readonly GraphServiceClient _graphClient;

    public ProfileController(GraphServiceClient graphClient)
    {
        _graphClient = graphClient;
    }

    public async Task<IActionResult> Index()
    {
        // Call Microsoft Graph
        var user = await _graphClient.Me.GetAsync();
        return View(user);
    }
}
```
*(Source: [learn.microsoft.com/entra/msidweb/call-downstream-apis/microsoft-graph](https://learn.microsoft.com/entra/msidweb/call-downstream-apis/microsoft-graph#use-graphserviceclient))*

### Summary
- **Package:** `Microsoft.Graph`
- **Main class to instantiate:** `GraphServiceClient` — this is the fluent client that exposes the entire Graph API surface (e.g., `graphClient.Me`, `graphClient.Users`, `graphClient.Me.Messages`, etc.).
- **Call pattern:** `graphClient.Me.GetAsync()` maps directly to the REST call `GET https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/me`.
- The `GraphServiceClient` is typically constructed with a `TokenCredential` (e.g., via Azure Identity or Microsoft.Identity.Web) that supplies the OAuth 2.0 [REDACTED] needed to authenticate the Graph request, and is commonly registered via dependency injection in ASP.NET Core apps.
